description: Vue 3.5+ and Nuxt UI v4 component conventions
globs: "*.vue"
alwaysApply: false
---

You are an expert in TypeScript, Vue 3.5, Nuxt 4, Nuxt UI v4, Pinia, VueUse, and Tailwind CSS v4.

Code Style and Structure

- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples.
- Use composition API and declarative programming patterns; never use options API.
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication.
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError).
- Structure files: exported component, composables, pinia stores, helpers, interfaces, types.
- Prefer using global pinia store.

Naming Conventions

- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard).
- Use PascalCase for component names (e.g., AuthWizard.vue).
- Use camelCase for composables (e.g., useAuthState.ts).
- Use camelCase for stores (e.g., authStore.ts).

Import Aliases (Nuxt 4)

Nuxt 4 changed `~` and `@` from rootDir (Nuxt 3) to `app/`. **`~/server/...` is broken; use `#server/...` or a relative path.**

| Alias | Resolves to | Typical use |
|---|---|---|
| `~` / `@` | `app/` | Components, composables, pages, utils, assets |
| `~~` / `@@` | `<rootDir>` | Rarely needed; prefer a more specific alias |
| `#shared` | `shared/` | Code shared between client and server (types, pure helpers) |
| `#server` | `server/` | Importing server-side types from shared/app code |
| `assets` | `app/assets/` | Asset imports (CSS, images referenced from templates) |
| `public` | `public/` | Public assets served as-is |
| `#build` | `.nuxt/` | Generated build artefacts (read-only) |

- Frontend → frontend: `~/components/Foo.vue`, `~/composables/useBar.ts`.
- Frontend → server types only: `import type { … } from '#server/…'`.
- Server → server: `#server/utils/auth`, not relative paths. Exception: standalone scripts (e.g. `server/database/seed.ts`, run via `tsx` or `node` directly) must keep relative imports because the runtime can't resolve Nuxt aliases before `.nuxt/` is generated.
- Never import server runtime code from frontend files.

TypeScript Usage

- Use TypeScript for all code.
- Use Vue 3 with TypeScript, leveraging defineComponent and PropType.
- Prefer await, catch and throw over try catch

Syntax and Formatting

- Use arrow functions for methods and computed properties.
- Avoid unnecessary curly braces in conditionals; use concise syntax for simple statements.
- Use template syntax for declarative rendering.

UI and Styling

- Use Nuxt UI and Tailwind for components and styling.
- Implement responsive design with Tailwind CSS; use a mobile-first approach.
- Use design tokens from `@theme` in `main.css`; never hardcode colors, radius, or shadows. Use Tailwind classes (`rounded-md`, `shadow-sm`, `text-primary-500`) that reference the tokens.
- Change brand colors in `app/app.config.ts` under `ui.colors`, not in CSS.
- Use the `<UIcon />` component for icons: it resolves any Iconify set on demand via the `<prefix>:<name>` naming convention, e.g.:

```vue
<UIcon name="lucide:house" />
<UIcon name="material-symbols:10k-sharp" />
```

`lucide` is the icon set already used throughout this project's own components; prefer it unless a page already commits to another set. If `package.json#dependencies` has `@fortawesome/*` packages (the optional FontAwesome Pro feature), `fas:`/`far:`/etc. prefixes are also available; check for those packages before reaching for a FontAwesome prefix, since they aren't installed by default.

NuxtUI specific

- When using the `UTable` component make sure you:
    - make sure to import the correct types for a table `import type { TableRow, TableData, TableColumn, TableOptions, TableProps } from '@nuxt/ui'`
    - always use the slots pattern `<template #XXX-cell="{ cell, column, getValue, renderValue, row, table }">` for example
    ```vue
<script setup lang="ts">
    import type { TableColumn, DropdownMenuItem } from '@nuxt/ui'
    import { useClipboard } from '@vueuse/core'

    interface User {
        id: number
        name: string
        position: string
        email: string
        role: string
    }

    const toast = useToast()
    const { copy } = useClipboard()

    const data = ref<User[]>([
        {
            id: 1,
            name: 'Lindsay Walton',
            position: 'Front-end Developer',
            email: 'lindsay.walton@example.com',
            role: 'Member',
        },
        {
            id: 2,
            name: 'Courtney Henry',
            position: 'Designer',
            email: 'courtney.henry@example.com',
            role: 'Admin',
        },
        {
            id: 3,
            name: 'Tom Cook',
            position: 'Director of Product',
            email: 'tom.cook@example.com',
            role: 'Member',
        },
        {
            id: 4,
            name: 'Whitney Francis',
            position: 'Copywriter',
            email: 'whitney.francis@example.com',
            role: 'Admin',
        },
        {
            id: 5,
            name: 'Leonard Krasner',
            position: 'Senior Designer',
            email: 'leonard.krasner@example.com',
            role: 'Owner',
        },
        {
            id: 6,
            name: 'Floyd Miles',
            position: 'Principal Designer',
            email: 'floyd.miles@example.com',
            role: 'Member',
        },
    ])

    const columns: TableColumn<User>[] = [
        {
            accessorKey: 'id',
            header: 'ID',
        },
        {
            accessorKey: 'name',
            header: 'Name',
        },
        {
            accessorKey: 'email',
            header: 'Email',
        },
        {
            accessorKey: 'role',
            header: 'Role',
        },
        {
            id: 'action',
        },
    ]

    function getDropdownActions(user: User): DropdownMenuItem[][] {
        return [
            [
                {
                    label: 'Copy user Id',
                    icon: 'i-lucide-copy',
                    onSelect: () => {
                        copy(user.id.toString())

                        toast.add({
                            title: 'User ID copied to clipboard!',
                            color: 'success',
                            icon: 'i-lucide-circle-check',
                        })
                    },
                },
            ],
            [
                {
                    label: 'Edit',
                    icon: 'i-lucide-edit',
                },
                {
                    label: 'Delete',
                    icon: 'i-lucide-trash',
                    color: 'error',
                },
            ],
        ]
    }
</script>

<template>
    <UTable :data="data" :columns="columns" class="flex-1">
        <template #name-cell="{ row }">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                <UAvatar :src="`https://i.pravatar.cc/120?img=${row.original.id}`" size="lg" :alt="`${row.original.name} avatar`" />
                <div>
                    <p class="font-medium text-highlighted">
                        {{ row.original.name }}
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        {{ row.original.position }}
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </template>
        <template #action-cell="{ row }">
            <UDropdownMenu :items="getDropdownActions(row.original)">
                <UButton icon="i-lucide-ellipsis-vertical" color="neutral" variant="ghost" aria-label="Actions" />
            </UDropdownMenu>
        </template>
    </UTable>
</template>
    ```
- When using the `UModal` component make sure you:
    - use the `title` prop for the modal header and `#body` slot for content (NOT `#header`/`#footer` slots)
    - optionally use the `description` prop for subtitle text
    ```vue
    <UModal v-model:open="open" title="My Modal" description="Optional description">
        <template #body>
            <!-- modal content here -->
        </template>
    </UModal>
    ```
- When using the `UDropdownMenu` component:
    - define items as `DropdownMenuItem[][]` (array of groups)
    - use `onSelect` callback on each item for actions
    ```vue
    <UDropdownMenu :items="dropdownItems">
        <UButton icon="i-lucide-ellipsis-vertical" color="neutral" variant="ghost" />
    </UDropdownMenu>
    ```
- When using the `USelectMenu` component:
    - use `value-key` prop to return the value string instead of the whole object
    ```vue
    <USelectMenu v-model="selected" :items="options" value-key="value" />
    ```

Calendar / Date Picker

- NEVER use the Nuxt UI calendar component.
- Always use `@vuepic/vue-datepicker` for all date/calendar needs.
- Use the `<ExampleCalendar>` component or import `VueDatePicker` directly.
- Docs: https://vue3datepicker.com/

Key Conventions

- Use VueUse for common composables and utility functions.
- Use Pinia for UI state management (modals, sidebar, preferences).
- Use `$fetch` / `useFetch` for server data operations.

Vue 3 and Composition API Best Practices

- Vue components have the following order <template> <script>.
- Prefer not to use <style> but instead use tailwind classes.
- Use <script setup> syntax for concise component definitions.
- Always prefer ref over reactive.
- Leverage ref, and computed for reactive state management.
- Prever using global pinia store over provide/inject.
- Implement custom composables for reusable logic.

Follow the official Vue.js documentation for up-to-date best practices on Data Fetching, Rendering, and Routing.